# Core standards

These rules apply to every file in `backend/` and `frontend/`, and to docs, commit messages and chat replies.

## 1. Writing style

Must:
- Use a period or a comma where you would reach for a dash.

Never:
- Write an em dash (`—`) anywhere. That includes code, comments, JSDoc, UI copy, docs, commit messages and chat replies.
- Swap in an en dash (`–`) or a spaced hyphen (` - `) as a dash substitute.

```ts
// Bad
/** Returns the user — or null if the account was deleted. */

// Good
/** Returns the user, or null if the account was deleted. */
```

## 2. Comments and JSDoc

A comment exists to save the next reader time. It states something the code can't show, such as a business rule, a constraint, a security reason, a block of code or a performance choice.

Must:
- Add JSDoc to every exported helper, hook, guard, decorator, pipe and interceptor.
- Add JSDoc to service methods when the name doesn't tell the whole story. Examples are methods that throw on purpose, mutate several tables, or have ordering rules.
- In JSDoc, say what the function returns, when it throws and what the caller must respect. Use `@param` only when a parameter's meaning isn't obvious from its name and type.
- Put a short comment above a block when it encodes a rule a reader couldn't infer. Examples are "Refresh tokens are hashed with SHA-256 because bcrypt truncates input at 72 bytes" and "Count runs in the same transaction so totals match the page".

Never:
- Restate the code (`// loop over users`, `// call the service`, `// return the result`).
- Talk to a reviewer (`// fixed`, `// changed from X`, `// as requested`, `// new helper`).
- Leave commented-out code. Delete it, since git keeps the history.
- Write a `TODO` without a reason. Use `// TODO: <what and why>`.
- Add a JSDoc block that only repeats the function name (`/** Gets the user. */` on `getUser`).

```ts
// Bad
// Get users
const users = await this.prisma.withSoftDelete.user.findMany({ where, select });

// Good
/**
 * Lists users for the admin table.
 * Throws BadRequestException when `sortBy` is not in USER_SORTABLE_FIELDS.
 * Never returns password or token hashes.
 */
async listForAdmin(query: ListUsersQueryDto): Promise<PaginatedResult<AdminUserListItem>> {
```

## 3. No magic strings for domain values

Domain values are roles, statuses, action types, sort fields, cookie names, query keys, route paths and storage folders. Compare, assign and branch on them through constants only.

Must:
- On the backend, use Prisma enums from `@prisma/client` for values stored in the database. Put every other domain value in an `as const` object in the module's `constants/` folder, or in `shared/constants/` when several modules use it.
- On the frontend, mirror Prisma enums with the same string values in `src/constants/`, and derive types in `src/types/`.
- Name constant objects and their keys in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE.
- Derive types from the constant. Never write a separate string union.

Never:
- Compare against a raw string (`user.role === 'SUPER_ADMIN'`).
- Define a constant object inside a `types/` file, or a type inside a `constants/` file.

```ts
// frontend/src/constants/user.ts
export const USER_STATUS = {
  ACTIVE: 'ACTIVE',
  SUSPENDED: 'SUSPENDED',
} as const;

// frontend/src/types/user.ts
import type { USER_STATUS } from '@/constants';
export type UserStatus = (typeof USER_STATUS)[keyof typeof USER_STATUS];

// Bad
if (user.status === 'SUSPENDED') {}

// Good
if (user.status === USER_STATUS.SUSPENDED) {}
```

Out of scope: user-facing copy, log messages, exception messages, database table and column names, and import paths. On the frontend, user-facing copy goes in copy constants (see `frontend-architecture.mdc`).

## 4. Module structure and barrels

Must:
- Follow the folder trees in `docs/PLAN.md` sections 3.2 and 4.2. Ask before adding a new top-level folder.
- Keep a backend module's `<feature>.module.ts`, `<feature>.controller.ts` and service files at the module root.
- Give each subfolder (`dto/`, `helpers/`, `constants/`, `types/`) an `index.ts` barrel that re-exports that folder only.
- Import a subfolder through its barrel (`../users/dto`), never a deep file path inside it.
- Keep `constants/` to `as const` objects and literal values, `types/` to types and interfaces, and `helpers/` and `utils/` to functions.
- Start new constants, types or helpers in the folder's `index.ts` or one existing file. Split into a sibling file when the file passes about 200 lines or holds two unrelated concerns.

Never:
- Create a barrel at a module root.
- Create circular imports between barrels.
- Create one file per tiny constant.

## 5. Reuse helpers before writing new ones

Both apps ship a large helper library. Writing a duplicate is a bug.

Must:
- Before writing a helper, search `backend/src/modules/shared/` (`utils/`, `helpers/`, `decorators/`, `pipes/`, `validators/`) or `frontend/src/lib/` and `frontend/src/hooks/`.
- When an existing helper is close but not right, extend it with an option instead of writing a second one.
- Put a new helper that two or more modules need in `shared/` (backend) or `lib/` (frontend), with JSDoc and a unit test on the backend.

Helpers people forget most often:

| Need | Backend | Frontend |
|---|---|---|
| Offset pagination | `buildPaginationArgs`, `buildPaginatedResult` | `getPageNumbers`, `getPaginationRange`, `DataTablePagination` |
| Cursor pagination | `buildCursorArgs`, `buildCursorResult` | `DataTableLoadMore`, `infiniteQueryOptions` factories |
| Search and sort | `buildSearchWhere`, `buildOrderBy` | `useDebouncedValue`, `useTableUrlState` |
| PATCH payloads | `buildPatchData` | `omitEmptyStringEntries` |
| Required filters | `requireDefined` | none |
| Dates | `getDateRange`, `TIME_FILTERS`, `zonedDateTimeToUtc` | `formatDate`, date-range query builder |
| Hashing | `hashData`, `compareData`, `hashToken` | none |
| Booleans from strings | `parseBoolean` | `parseBoolean` |
| Prisma errors | `isPrismaUniqueConstraintError`, `toUserConflictException` | none |
| Redirect safety | none | `sanitizeRedirectPath` |
| Class names | none | `cn` |

## 6. TypeScript

Must:
- Keep `strict` on. Use `unknown` and narrow it instead of `any`.
- Use `import type` for type-only imports.
- Make `switch` statements over enums exhaustive with a `default` branch that assigns to `never`.
- Give exported functions explicit return types.

Never:
- Use the non-null assertion `!`, unless a check the compiler can't see guarantees the value. Add a comment naming that check.
- Use `as` to silence a type error. Fix the type instead.
- Use `@ts-ignore`. Use `@ts-expect-error` with a reason if you have no other option.

## 7. Tooling

Must:
- Leave every change passing `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck` and Prettier in the app you touched.
- Run `npm run lint:fix` and `npm run format` before calling a task done.

Never:
- Change ESLint, Prettier or `tsconfig` settings to make an error go away.
- Add `eslint-disable` without a reason after `--` (`// eslint-disable-next-line rule-name -- reason`).
- Use `console.log`. Use the Nest `Logger` on the backend. On the frontend, remove debug logging before finishing.

## Review checklist

- [ ] No em dashes or dash substitutes anywhere in the diff.
- [ ] Every exported helper, hook, guard and decorator has JSDoc that adds information.
- [ ] No comment restates code or talks to a reviewer. No commented-out code.
- [ ] Domain values come from Prisma enums or `as const` objects. No raw string comparisons.
- [ ] New files sit in the folders from `docs/PLAN.md`, with barrels updated.
- [ ] No new helper duplicates an existing one.
- [ ] No `any`, `@ts-ignore`, unexplained `!` or `console.log`.
- [ ] Lint, typecheck and Prettier pass.
